    statement: "Delays adoption of newly published dependency versions by a set period so that compromised releases, which are usually pulled within hours or days, never reach a build.",
    origin: "Registry compromises in which a maintainer account was hijacked and a malicious version published were detected and removed within hours in several incidents (ua-parser-js in 2021, the self replicating npm worm campaigns of 2025), yet builds that resolved the newest version in that window were infected. Package managers responded by adding a minimum release age setting: pnpm introduced minimumReleaseAge and later made a one day default, and npm added an equivalent option. The practice generalises a long standing habit in operating system distributions of letting packages sit in a testing channel before promotion.",
    structure: "Dependency resolution is configured to ignore any version published less than N hours ago, with N typically between one and seven days depending on the ecosystem's detection speed. Lock files pin exact versions and hashes so builds do not drift; updates are proposed by a bot and pass through the same cooldown. Security fixes needing immediate adoption go through a documented exception that names the package, the version and the reason, and expires. Install scripts are disabled by default so even a dependency that passes the cooldown cannot run code at install time without an explicit allow list. The SBOM for each release shows which dependencies changed, and the cooldown is enforced in CI as well as on developer machines so a local override does not reach production. Monitoring of advisory feeds marks any version that is later yanked or flagged, and the cooldown's value is measured by how many such versions were never installed.",
